having marked a century since the end of  the great war of 1914 18 we now mark the  75th anniversary of the official end of  the 1939-45 war in Europe generally  known as VE Day the 8th of May 1945 was  the Allies formal acceptance of Nazi  Germany's unconditional surrender after  six years of conflict the British were  keen to party and countless numbers took  to the streets crowds massed in  Trafalgar Square the man and outside  Buckingham Palace where King George the  6th and Queen Elizabeth the Winston  Churchill appeared on the balcony in  London alone two and a half million teas  and lemonade's were consumed two million  sandwiches and 1.2 million buns there  was a general air of joy and relief and  this was not just restricted to the  Capitol here in Malden  locals had experienced the impact of the  hostilities firsthand loved ones had  gone off to fight some of them never to  return there are 86 names on our towns  war memorial alone locals did their bit  in the Civil Defence services they  experienced the hardships of rationing  some of that rationing would continue  until 1954 and air raids have been a  regular feature of life and death  there were 14 fatalities in Malden and  Highbridge and they've been so much more  going on besides so when they heard the  prime minister's broadcast on their  primitive crystal wireless sets and  realized that the blackouts could at  large long last come down that there'll  be no further need for them to rush to  their Morrison shelters they knew that  this really was their victory as  Churchill told them in all our long  history we've never seen a greater day  than this the residents of individual  streets in Malden Burnham and all across  the surrounding village  hurriedly made plans to put up bunting  and flags and to roll down a barrel or  two to capture the moment for fear that  it was only a dream supplies were pulled  an imaginative feasts were concocted  spam fritters and powdered eggs were  very much in evidence and then there  were the famous Walton pies named after  Lord Walton the minister of food and  consisting of vegetables in a brown  flour marjorine pastry moistened with  Bovril gravy  there were parties bonfires including a  massive one on the promenade which burnt  all night and dancing not least outside  of the Swan Hotel in Milton's High  Street the modern pubs ignored all of  the licensing hours and they were packed  but there was no trouble at all and one  particular participant remembers it was  a delightful day and one which I will  always remember and talking about  remembering and remembrance we must rely  very heavily on personal accounts of  what happened that day because it seems  that very few official records survived  photographs do however capture the  moment and there are some wonderful  pictures of the partygoers in places  like Fitch's Crescent  North streets and outside what was then  the Salvation Army Hall now the mosque  in wilden's Church Street when you look  at those pictures some of the adults are  wearing black respecting the absence of  husbands and sweethearts who made the  ultimate sacrifice on this the 75th  anniversary of d-day whilst we've not  been able to mark the event across the  district and as we'd originally planned  I hope that you will still take some  time to join in on the many online  activities to mark this very special  occasion 75 years since VE Day 75 years  of peace  we meet again don't know  [Music]
 

